The deep integration of neuroscience and artificial intelligence (AI) is catalyzing a paradigm shift in computation \cite{ref1,ref2,ref7, zhang2024brain}. For decades, AI development has largely followed a computer science-driven path, achieving remarkable success but also encountering fundamental limitations in energy efficiency, adaptability, and general intelligence \cite{ref6,ref94}. A promising alternative, brain-inspired AI (BIAI), is emerging. This approach seeks not merely to replicate the brain's behavioral outputs (the \textit{what}) but to understand and implement its underlying structural and computational principles (the \textit{how}) \cite{ref1,ref10}. By drawing inspiration from the brain's architecture, learning rules, and dynamic processes, BIAI aims to overcome critical challenges such as the von Neumann bottleneck and catastrophic forgetting \cite{ref4,ref5,ref6,ref9}, paving the way toward more robust and capable artificial general intelligence \cite{ref1,ref7,ref8}.

Recent progress in BIAI has established a foundational blueprint for intelligence, advancing from simulating individual neural components to constructing self-organizing network structures. A pivotal step has been the shift from abstract artificial neural networks (ANNs) to more biologically plausible and energy-efficient Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs), which compute using discrete, event-driven spikes \cite{ref9, ref13, ref14, ref28, ref36}. Initially hampered by training difficulties, SNNs have recently achieved significant performance breakthroughs. A prime example is the Spikformer, a novel framework that successfully integrates the self-attention mechanism of Transformers with the temporal dynamics of SNNs, demonstrating state-of-the-art performance on complex image classification tasks with substantially lower energy consumption \cite{ref11}. This fusion of mainstream AI architectures with SNNs is rapidly expanding to other domains, including 3D point cloud processing and image segmentation \cite{ref15, ref16, ref27, ref30}. This progress is underpinned by algorithmic innovations, such as surrogate gradient methods and brain-inspired chaotic backpropagation, that effectively address the challenge of training networks with non-differentiable spike events \cite{ref13, ref14, ref17, ref18}. Beyond individual neurons, intelligence emerges from their intricate connectivity \cite{ref31, ref32}. Drawing inspiration from the brain's ability to self-organize, researchers have developed a brain-inspired neural circuit evolution framework (NeuEvo) \cite{ref12}. This framework allows an SNN to autonomously evolve its own circuitry, dynamically generating canonical motifs like feedforward excitation, feedback inhibition, and lateral inhibition based on local spike-timing-dependent plasticity (STDP) rules \cite{ref19, ref20, ref32, ref33, ref34}. This self-evolving capability not only enhances network performance but also represents a crucial move away from handcrafted architectures toward more adaptive and biologically realistic designs \cite{ref23, ref24, ref35}.

A static network architecture, however sophisticated, is insufficient for genuine intelligence. The brain’s remarkable adaptability stems from its dynamic regulation through neuromodulatory systems, which act as a \textit{software} layer controlling learning and information flow \cite{ref42, ref49, ref52}. Researchers are now beginning to inject this \textit{soul} into AI. A key breakthrough is the development of a multi-neuromodulatory computational model inspired by the roles of dopamine, acetylcholine, serotonin, and noradrenaline \cite{ref37}. This model dynamically regulates synaptic plasticity to overcome catastrophic forgetting—a critical flaw in traditional ANNs—dramatically improving performance in continual learning scenarios \cite{ref38, ref39, ref43, ref44, ref45, ref46}. This approach is deeply rooted in neuroscientific findings, such as the role of dopamine in encoding reward prediction errors for reinforcement learning \cite{ref40, ref41, ref53} and norepinephrine in modulating the trade-off between exploration and exploitation \cite{ref51, ref54}. By translating these biological principles into computational mechanisms, AI models are gaining the ability to adapt their learning rules based on context, uncertainty, and task demands, mirroring the flexible cognitive control observed in humans \cite{ref52, ref57} and reflecting the complex brain dynamics seen in various cognitive states \cite{ref55, ref56, ref78}.

To fully realize the potential of BIAI algorithms, the journey must extend from simulation to physical reality, requiring both novel hardware substrates and ultimate human-machine interfaces. The limitations of conventional von Neumann computing have spurred the development of neuromorphic hardware, which mimics the brain's in-memory and parallel processing capabilities \cite{ref4, ref62}. Memristors, with their ability to emulate the plasticity of biological synapses, have emerged as a leading candidate for building ultra-low-power neuromorphic systems \cite{ref58, ref89}. This has culminated in large-scale neuromorphic processors like Intel's Loihi, Tsinghua University's Tianjic and Tianmouc chips, and Zhejiang University's Darwin series, which can execute SNNs with orders-of-magnitude greater energy efficiency than conventional hardware \cite{ref60, ref61, ref69, ref70, ref71, wu2024adaptive}. The most ambitious frontier of BIAI is closing the loop between artificial systems and the biological brain \cite{ref63, ref79}. A groundbreaking recent study demonstrated a memristor-based adaptive neuromorphic decoder that forms a co-adaptive, bidirectional learning loop with the human brain \cite{ref59}. In this framework, the memristor chip deciphers brain signals while simultaneously adapting to neural fluctuations, and the brain, in turn, adapts to the decoder, leading to a synergistic co-evolution that significantly enhances brain-computer interface (BCI) performance \cite{ref64, ref65, ref66}. This represents a paradigm shift from machines that passively interpret brain signals to systems that actively and intelligently collaborate with the brain, with profound implications for neurorehabilitation and augmenting human capabilities \cite{ref76, ref77, ref81}.

Despite these significant advances, the path toward general intelligence is fraught with fundamental challenges. First is the persistent dilemma of balancing biological realism and computational efficiency; incorporating more complex neural dynamics, such as dendritic computation, often leads to an exponential increase in computational cost that current hardware cannot support \cite{ref86, ref91}. Second, the field faces deep algorithmic and training difficulties, particularly for SNNs. Current training methods often rely on surrogate gradients or conversion from pre-trained ANNs, which are not fully biologically plausible and may not harness the full potential of temporal spike-based computation \cite{ref13, ref86, ref87}. Third, a critical hardware-software co-design gap remains. Brain-inspired algorithms are often not optimized for the specific constraints and opportunities of neuromorphic hardware, while the hardware itself lacks the flexibility to support the diverse and dynamic models proposed by theorists \cite{ref72, ref85, ref88, ref93}. Finally, the field currently lacks a unified theoretical framework that can mathematically connect neural dynamics, learning rules, information processing, and metabolic costs, hindering the development of interpretable, predictable, and systematically optimized BIAI systems \cite{ref87, ref90, ref92}.

In summary, brain-inspired AI has evolved from a niche concept into a vibrant and rapidly advancing field. By simulating the brain's architecture, dynamics, and physical implementation, researchers are creating a new generation of intelligent systems that are more efficient, adaptive, and capable. This endeavor holds a dual promise: it is not only a powerful pathway to engineer superior AI but also a unique computational lens through which we can understand the nature of our own intelligence \cite{ref3, ref98, ref104}. The future of AI will not be forged within a single discipline. Instead, it demands a deep, interdisciplinary fusion of neuroscience, computer science, materials science, and cognitive science to bridge the existing gaps and unlock new frontiers \cite{ref95, ref96, ref100, ref101}. By fostering this collaboration, we can accelerate the translation of BIAI from foundational research to real-world applications, heralding a new era of human-AI co-evolution and intelligent technology \cite{ref97, ref99, ref102}.
